Below is a requirements document you can give to GitHub Copilot to build Resilio inside the AmeriHealth environment. I’m defining Resilio as an internal quality-engineering portal that manages API test execution, Jenkins integration, live status, reports, KPI tracking, and AI-assisted failure analysis.

Resilio — Product Requirements

1. Purpose

Resilio will provide a single internal portal for teams to:

* Start automated API tests.
* Run tests by project, environment, suite, tag, or test-case ID.
* Monitor execution in real time.
* View request, response, pass, fail, and skipped results.
* Download execution reports.
* Track automation KPIs.
* Analyze failures.
* Compare results across builds.
* Integrate with Jenkins, Git repositories, databases, and the existing Java API automation framework.

Resilio must run entirely within AmeriHealth-approved infrastructure.

2. Primary Users

* Quality engineers
* Automation engineers
* Developers
* QA managers
* Product teams
* Release managers
* Application support teams
* Administrators

3. Roles and Permissions

Administrator

* Manage users and roles.
* Register projects.
* Configure environments.
* Configure Jenkins and repository connections.
* Manage secrets references.
* View audit logs.
* Retain or archive execution records.

Automation Engineer

* Configure test suites.
* Start executions.
* Upload test data.
* View detailed requests and responses.
* Rerun failed tests.
* Manage test-case metadata.

QA Engineer

* Start approved tests.
* Monitor executions.
* View and download reports.
* Rerun failed tests.
* View failure analysis.

Manager/Viewer

* View dashboards, trends, and reports.
* Filter results by project, application, release, or date.
* Cannot change configurations or execute restricted tests.

4. Functional Requirements

4.1 Authentication

* Integrate with AmeriHealth SSO.
* Support approved enterprise identity providers.
* Enforce role-based access control.
* Automatically end inactive sessions.
* Do not store user passwords.
* Record login and access events in audit logs.

4.2 Project Management

Each project must contain:

* Project name
* Product or business area
* Application name
* Repository location
* Default branch
* Jenkins job name
* Framework location
* Available environments
* Available test suites
* Project owners
* Notification group
* Active/inactive status

Users must only see projects they are authorized to access.

4.3 Environment Configuration

Support environments such as:

* Development
* QA
* SIT
* UAT
* Performance
* Production, if explicitly authorized

Each environment may define:

* Base API URL
* Authentication type
* OAuth configuration reference
* SSL certificate reference
* Database configuration reference
* Response-time threshold
* Jenkins agent label
* Environment-specific properties

Secrets, tokens, passwords, and certificate passcodes must never be stored as plain text in Resilio.

4.4 Test Execution

Users must be able to start a test using:

* Project
* Environment
* Git branch
* Run type
* Suite
* Cucumber tag
* Single test-case ID
* Multiple test-case IDs
* Smoke tests
* Regression tests
* Negative tests
* Master suite
* Failed-test rerun

Optional execution inputs:

* Release number
* Build number
* Commit ID
* Defect number
* Test-data file
* Payload file
* Test-case JSON file
* Custom runtime parameters
* PCP flag or similar project-specific flags

Resilio must validate all inputs before starting Jenkins.

4.5 File Upload

Allow approved file types only:

* .json
* .csv
* .txt
* .yaml
* .yml

Requirements:

* Enforce configurable file-size limits.
* Scan files using AmeriHealth-approved malware scanning.
* Validate JSON and YAML syntax.
* Reject executable or unsupported files.
* Store files only in approved internal locations.
* Do not expose server-side paths to users.
* Delete temporary files after the configured retention period.
* Record uploader, timestamp, project, and execution ID.

GET tests must not require a payload file.

4.6 Jenkins Integration

Resilio must:

* Start an approved Jenkins job through the Jenkins API.
* Pass execution parameters securely.
* Capture the Jenkins queue ID and build number.
* Track queued, running, completed, aborted, and failed states.
* Display the Jenkins build URL.
* Stream or periodically retrieve approved console output.
* Wait for the complete execution before finalizing results.
* Allow authorized users to stop a running build.
* Prevent duplicate execution requests caused by repeated clicks.
* Handle Jenkins outages and API timeouts gracefully.

Example Jenkins parameters:

* PROJECT
* PRODUCT
* ENVIRONMENT
* BRANCH
* RUN_TYPE
* TAGS
* TEST_CASE_IDS
* PAYLOAD_PATH
* DATA_FILE_PATH
* OAUTH2_ENABLED
* SSL_ENABLED
* DB_VALIDATION_ENABLED
* PCP_ENABLED
* REQUESTED_BY
* RESILIO_EXECUTION_ID

4.7 Live Execution Dashboard

Display:

* Execution ID
* Project
* Environment
* Suite
* Requested by
* Start time
* Current duration
* Current test case
* Total test cases
* Completed percentage
* Passed count
* Failed count
* Skipped count
* Currently running count
* Queued count
* Jenkins build status

Execution states:

* Created
* Validating
* Queued
* Running
* Passed
* Failed
* Partially passed
* Aborted
* Timed out
* Infrastructure error

The dashboard should refresh without requiring a full browser reload.

4.8 Parallel Execution

* Allow suites to run in parallel when supported by Jenkins and the framework.
* Display each parallel worker separately.
* Aggregate worker results into one execution.
* Prevent duplicate counting.
* Distinguish test failures from worker or infrastructure failures.
* Make concurrency configurable by project and environment.

4.9 Test Results

Capture results at the scenario or test-case level:

* Test-case ID
* Scenario name
* Tags
* Suite
* Start and end time
* Duration
* Status
* HTTP method
* Endpoint, with sensitive values masked
* Response status code
* Validation results
* Failure message
* Stack trace, if permitted
* Retry count
* Jenkins build number
* Git commit
* Report location

Test details should show:

* Request headers with secrets masked
* Request body with sensitive fields masked
* Response headers with sensitive fields masked
* Response body with PHI/PII masking
* Database validation results
* Expected versus actual values
* Execution logs

4.10 Reporting

Support:

* Extent HTML report
* Cucumber JSON report
* JUnit XML report
* Resilio summary report
* Downloadable CSV export

Reports must remain associated with their original execution ID.

Users should be able to filter by:

* Date range
* Project
* Application
* Environment
* Release
* Branch
* Suite
* Status
* Test-case ID
* Requested by

4.11 Failed-Test Rerun

* Allow rerunning all failed tests from an execution.
* Allow selecting individual failed tests.
* Link rerun results to the original execution.
* Show original status and rerun status.
* Do not replace the original result.
* Track how many attempts were required.
* Distinguish stable failures from flaky tests.

4.12 KPI Dashboard

Display:

* Total executions
* Total test cases
* Pass rate
* Failure rate
* Skipped rate
* Automation coverage
* Average execution time
* Time saved through automation
* Flaky-test rate
* Rerun success rate
* Failures by application
* Failures by environment
* Failures by test suite
* Execution trend by day, week, and release
* Top recurring failures
* Infrastructure failure rate

KPI records should include:

* Execution ID
* Project
* Environment
* Suite
* Build number
* Jenkins URL
* Git branch
* Commit ID
* Release number
* Total
* Passed
* Failed
* Skipped
* Duration
* Requested by
* Start and completion timestamps

4.13 Failure Classification

Classify failures as:

* Assertion failure
* API functional failure
* Authentication failure
* SSL/certificate failure
* Test-data failure
* Database validation failure
* Schema validation failure
* Response-time failure
* Framework failure
* Jenkins failure
* Environment failure
* Network failure
* Unknown failure

Users with permission should be able to correct the classification.

4.14 AI-Assisted Analysis

If AmeriHealth approves AI use, Resilio may provide:

* Failure summary in plain language.
* Suggested probable cause.
* Similar previous failures.
* Recommended investigation steps.
* Log summarization.
* Detection of recurring failure patterns.
* Suggested defect description.

AI requirements:

* Use only AmeriHealth-approved models and endpoints.
* Do not send PHI, PII, secrets, tokens, or unrestricted logs to an external service.
* Mask sensitive content before submitting it to a model.
* Label AI-generated information clearly.
* Require human review.
* Do not allow AI to modify production data or approve releases.
* Record when AI analysis was requested and by whom.

